course: ukrainian-street
lesson: 12
title: Drinks, sweets and "enjoy your meal"
level: primary-4
stars: 1
explain: Before eating, someone at the table says SMAchnoho! — enjoy your meal — and the answer is DYAkuyu, i vam!
explain: The best thing a guest can say about the food is SMAchno, tasty; the cook will smile and probably give you more.
explain: In a Ukrainian home a guest is almost always offered chai, just tea, often black with sugar or lemon, and Granny's bowl of tsukErky, wrapped sweets.
explain: Watch the sound-alikes: sik sounds like «sick» but means juice, tort is a cake and not a legal word, and ya hoLOdnyi (I'm hungry) is only one sound away from khoLOdnyi (cold).
fact: chai means tea, offered to guests always; it is usually black tea with sugar or lemon, not spiced chai.
fact: vodA means water, stressed at the end; when asking you say mozhna vodu?
fact: sik means juice, even though it sounds like the English «sick».
fact: molokO means milk, stressed at the end.
fact: tsukErky are sweets or candies; grannies press them on guests, and one polite DYAkuyu is enough.
fact: morOzyvo means ice cream, literally something like «frozen thing».
fact: pechYvo means biscuits, eaten with tea.
fact: tort means a big layered cake, for example for birthdays.
fact: SMAchnoho! means enjoy your meal, said before eating, and the answer is DYAkuyu, i vam!
fact: SMAchno means tasty or delicious; leaving the table without a DYAkuyu after the meal looks rude.
fact: ya hoLOdnyi means I'm hungry, said by a boy, and a girl says ya hoLOdna; khoLOdnyi, which means cold, sounds very close.
fact: ya nayIvsya means I'm full, I've eaten enough, the polite way to refuse more; a granny may still offer more, so say it kindly and firmly.
exercise: What is chai in a Ukrainian home? | Ordinary tea, often black with sugar or lemon | Spiced milky chai with cinnamon, cardamom and ginger | A cold fizzy drink | Hot chocolate
exercise: What does vodA mean? | Water | Vodka | Juice | Food
exercise: How do you ask for water? | mozhna vodu? | vodA tobI? | SMAchnoho vodA | ya vodA
exercise: What does sik mean? | Juice | Feeling sick | Six, the number | Hot soup
exercise: What does molokO mean? | Milk | Honey | Water | Cocoa
exercise: What are tsukErky? | Sweets, candies | Sugar cubes only | Biscuits | Cakes
exercise: Granny offers tsukErky again and again. What is enough? | One polite DYAkuyu | Thanking her ten times | Eating the whole bowl | Saying nothing at all
exercise: What does morOzyvo mean? | Ice cream | Frozen peas | Snow | Lolly stick
exercise: What is pechYvo? | Biscuits | Thin pancakes | Bread rolls | Meat pies
exercise: What is a tort? | A big layered cake | A tart with fruit on top | A legal case | A sandwich
exercise: When do people say SMAchnoho!? | Before eating | After a goal | At bedtime | When someone sneezes
exercise: How do you answer SMAchnoho!? | DYAkuyu, i vam! | SMAchno! | ya nayIvsya, DYAkuyu | BUvai!
exercise: What does SMAchno mean? | Tasty, delicious | Smelly, a bit off | Too hot | Not enough
exercise: What looks rude at the end of a meal? | Leaving the table without saying DYAkuyu | Saying SMAchno | Asking for water | Eating everything
exercise: What does ya hoLOdnyi mean? | I'm hungry | I'm cold, freezing | I'm full up | I'm thirsty
exercise: Which word sounds very close to hoLOdnyi (hungry) but means cold? | khoLOdnyi | molokO | morOzyvo | holubtsI
exercise: What does ya nayIvsya mean? | I'm full, I've eaten enough | I'm hungry, what's for lunch? | I'm cold | I'm thirsty
test: «sik means sick, don't drink it» Which is right? | No, sik means juice | Yes, it's medicine for a cough | Yes, it means ill in bed | No, it means milk from a cow
test: Sashko's mum asks «chai?». You picture spicy milky chai. What will you probably get? | Ordinary tea, often black with sugar or lemon | Spicy milky chai | Coffee | Lemonade
test: You say «ya khoLOdnyi» when you want lunch. What might Sashko's mum think? | That you're cold, not hungry | That you're hungry and want food | That you're full | That you're tired
test: A girl wants food. What does she say? | ya hoLOdna | ya hoLOdnyi | ya hoLOdne | ya hoLOdni
test: Everyone sits down and Granny says «SMAchnoho!». What do you do? | Say DYAkuyu, i vam! and eat | Say ya nayIvsya and leave the table | Say do pobAchennya and wave goodbye | Say proBAch
test: What would happen if you jumped up and ran off after dinner without a word? | It would look rude; say DYAkuyu first | Nothing, everyone does that | Granny would clap | It would mean you loved the food
test: «tort means tart, like a jam tart» Which is right? | No, tort is a big layered cake | Yes, a small jam tart | Yes, a pie with apples inside | No, it's a pizza with cheese and ham
test: The cook asks how the borshch is. What makes her smile? | SMAchno! | nE-a | NOrmalno, I guess | ya hoLOdnyi
test: «morozyvo, sounds like moroz, frost… so it's ice?» What is right? | Close: it means ice cream, a «frozen thing» | Yes, it means plain ice cubes from the freezer for drinks | No, it means soup | No, it means snowman
test: «just say ya nayIvsya and she stops, easy» Which is more likely with a granny? | She may still offer more, so say it kindly and firmly | She stops at once and never asks again | She gets angry | She brings a bigger plate silently
test: In a game shop the Ukrainian label says molokO on a bottle of orange liquid. What is wrong? | molokO is milk, not orange juice | Nothing, molokO is orange juice in Ukrainian | molokO is water | molokO is tea
exam: Identify the formula pronounced before the commencement of a meal and the customary reply thereto. | SMAchnoho!, to which one replies DYAkuyu, i vam! | SMAchno!, to which one replies BUvai! | ya nayIvsya, to which one replies NIchoho | DObroi NOchi, to which one replies tak
exam: Select the response consistent with the established meaning of sik, notwithstanding its resemblance to an English word. | It signifies juice | It signifies illness | It signifies the number six | It signifies soup
exam: Which of the following statements most accurately characterises the distinction between hoLOdnyi and khoLOdnyi? | The former signifies hungry and the latter cold, the two differing by a single sound | Both signify hungry | The former signifies cold and the latter hungry | Both signify full
exam: Ascertain the conduct regarded as discourteous upon the conclusion of a meal. | Departure from the table without an expression of thanks | Commending the food as SMAchno | Requesting a glass of water | Consuming the whole portion
